-- Q4962: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The geographic locations where service organization operations occur","The scope of the engagement identifying included services, infrastructure, software, people, and procedures","The maximum number of user entities permitted to rely on the report findings","The time period during which the service auditor conducted fieldwork activities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): System boundaries in a SOC 2 report define the scope of the engagement by identifying which services, infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data are included in the examination. Per SSAE 18 (AT-C 320), the system description must clearly delineate what is covered so user entities understand the report''s applicability.
Wrong (A): Geographic locations may be mentioned in the system description but do not define system boundaries, which focus on services and components rather than physical locations.
Wrong (C): System boundaries do not limit how many user entities can rely on the report; any user entity may use a SOC report relevant to their outsourced services.
Wrong (D): The examination period is stated separately from system boundaries; boundaries define scope of services, not the timing of fieldwork.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4962;

-- Q4963: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service auditor found no exceptions or deviations in any controls tested during the period","The description is fairly presented and controls are effective except for specific identified matters","The service organization''s controls are completely ineffective across all trust service criteria","The service auditor was unable to perform sufficient testing procedures to form an opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A qualified opinion in a SOC 2 Type II report indicates that, except for specific matters described in the report, the system description is fairly presented and controls are suitably designed and operating effectively. Per SSAE 18 (AT-C 205), the qualification addresses limited exceptions rather than pervasive failures.
Wrong (A): No exceptions would result in an unqualified (clean) opinion, not a qualified one.
Wrong (C): Completely ineffective controls across all criteria would warrant an adverse opinion, not a qualification limited to specific matters.
Wrong (D): Inability to perform sufficient testing would result in a disclaimer of opinion, indicating the auditor cannot express an opinion at all.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4963;

-- Q4964: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service organization''s SOC report becomes invalid and must be reissued immediately","The related control objective may not be achieved even though the organization''s own controls operated effectively","The service auditor must revise the SOC report to remove the complementary user entity control","There is no consequence because complementary user entity controls are entirely voluntary"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): When a user entity fails to implement a complementary user entity control (CUEC) identified in a SOC report, the related control objective may not be fully achieved, even though the service organization''s own controls operated effectively. Per SSAE 18 (AT-C 320), CUECs represent controls the service organization assumes user entities will implement.
Wrong (A): The SOC report does not become invalid; it accurately describes the service organization''s controls regardless of user entity actions.
Wrong (C): The service auditor does not revise the report based on user entity behavior; CUECs are disclosed for user entities to implement at their discretion.
Wrong (D): While CUECs are not mandatory, failing to implement them can leave control gaps that affect the achievement of control objectives.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4964;

-- Q4965: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Type I, because it covers a longer examination period than Type II reports","Neither type — only SOC 3 reports provide assurance over operating effectiveness","Either type, because both Type I and Type II provide equivalent levels of assurance","Type II, because it includes testing of operating effectiveness over a specified period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A SOC 2 Type II report includes testing of the operating effectiveness of controls over a specified period, whereas a Type I report only addresses the suitability of design at a point in time. Per SSAE 18 (AT-C 320), Type II provides assurance that controls not only are designed properly but also functioned effectively throughout the review period.
Wrong (A): Type I covers a point in time, not a longer period; Type II covers a period and is therefore more comprehensive.
Wrong (B): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports come in Type I and Type II forms; SOC 3 is a general-use report with less detail, not the only source of effectiveness assurance.
Wrong (C): Type I and Type II provide different levels of assurance — Type II is more rigorous because it tests operating effectiveness over time.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4965;

-- Q5798: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Preventing unauthorized key rotation by rogue administrators within the organization","Enabling authorized decryption when the original key holder is unavailable or during legal proceedings","Eliminating the need for key management infrastructure and related operational overhead","Providing real-time key revocation across geographically distributed systems"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1, key escrow involves depositing a copy of cryptographic keys with a trusted third party to ensure authorized access when the primary key holder is unavailable, such as during employee termination or legal discovery. This addresses business continuity and regulatory compliance needs for data recovery.
Wrong (A): Key escrow does not prevent key rotation; it provides backup access to existing keys. Rotation controls are managed through key management policies, not escrow arrangements.
Wrong (C): Key escrow adds complexity to key management rather than eliminating it — the escrow agent itself requires secure storage, access controls, and audit procedures.
Wrong (D): Key escrow stores backup copies for recovery purposes; real-time revocation is handled by certificate revocation lists or online status protocols.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5798;

-- Q5859: Data Protection Technologies [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["File-level encryption targeting selected documents","Full disk encryption protecting the entire drive","Network-layer encryption securing data in transit","Database-level encryption for structured records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-111, full disk encryption (FDE) encrypts the entire contents of a storage device, including the operating system, applications, and temporary files. FDE provides comprehensive volume-level protection without requiring users to select individual files, addressing the lost-device threat scenario.
Wrong (A): File-level encryption only protects specifically designated files and may leave temporary files, swap space, and OS data exposed to an attacker with physical access.
Wrong (C): Network-layer encryption (such as TLS or IPsec) secures data in transit between systems but does not protect data stored on the laptop''s hard drive.
Wrong (D): Database-level encryption protects structured data within a database management system, not the entire hard drive contents including the OS and temp files.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5859;

-- Q5881: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The archived documents become permanently inaccessible once the old key is decommissioned","The previous key must be retained in a secure key store, increasing key management complexity","The archived documents automatically re-encrypt with the new key during the rotation process","The rotation has no impact because AES-256 keys are inherently backward compatible"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1, cryptographic key rotation does not automatically re-encrypt previously encrypted data. Organizations must retain expired keys in a secure key archive to maintain access to historical data, which increases key lifecycle management complexity and expands the scope of key protection responsibilities.
Wrong (A): Archived documents are not permanently lost; retaining the previous key in a secure archive preserves the ability to decrypt them when needed.
Wrong (C): Automatic re-encryption would require decrypting and re-encrypting all 2.3 million documents — a resource-intensive process that does not occur during standard key rotation.
Wrong (D): AES-256 keys are not backward compatible; each document can only be decrypted with the specific key used to encrypt it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5881;

-- Q5902: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["TDE significantly degrades query performance, making it unsuitable for production database workloads","TDE requires application code changes to encrypt and decrypt data during each query execution","TDE protects data at rest on disk but does not encrypt data in memory during query processing","TDE cannot be used with databases that contain more than 10 million individual records"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-111, Transparent Data Encryption encrypts database files at the storage layer, protecting against physical media theft. However, data is decrypted in memory when loaded for query processing, leaving it vulnerable to memory-scraping attacks by anyone with server-level access.
Wrong (A): TDE has minimal performance impact because encryption and decryption occur at the I/O level; Microsoft documentation confirms negligible overhead for most production workloads.
Wrong (B): TDE operates transparently below the application layer — applications read and write data normally without any code modifications, which is TDE''s primary operational advantage.
Wrong (D): TDE has no record-count limitation; it encrypts the entire database file regardless of the number of records stored within it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5902;

